<meta type=article title="CSV/TSV handlers + <code> long-form fixture (Phase 2 slice 2a)">
</meta>

<section | Standalone CSV>

The `<csv>` tag parses its content as comma-separated values and renders
a real HTML `<table>`. The handler reuses `table.js`'s `parseCsv` parser
and `renderParsedTable` builder, so `<csv>` and `<table csv | …>` produce
equivalent output for the same data.

<csv #csv:demo>
name,age,city
Alice,30,Boston
Bob,25,Seattle
Carol,42,Austin
</csv>

A CSV block with the default header-row treatment.

<section | Standalone TSV>

The `<tsv>` tag is the tab-separated counterpart.

<tsv>
fruit	color	count
apple	red	7
banana	yellow	12
</tsv>

<section | The `<code>` long-form bug fix>

Phase 0 of the Phase 2 handler bundle (commit `af7a0a3`) surfaced a
latent bug: `<code>` long-form was registered as
`interpreter_strategy: schema` while its DSL_REGISTRY entry made
content opaque. The schema dispatch dropped opaque content at
`convertContent`, so `<code | body>` rendered as an empty
`<code></code>` element with the pipe content silently lost. Phase 2
slice 2a added `handlers/code.js` (parallel to the existing
`inline-code.js`) and switched the vocab to handler dispatch. The
content now renders correctly:

<code language=python | def factorial(n): return 1 if n == 0 else n * factorial(n - 1)>

Two more `<code>` forms to confirm the handler covers the natural
shapes:

<code | x = 42>

The short-form-with-pipe-content above renders as a plain `<code>`
with no language class.

<code language=javascript | const sum = (a, b) => a + b>

And the language kwarg flows through to a `language-X` class on the
rendered `<code>` element, same as the inline-code sigil
`` <`language … `> ``.
